-- title:
--   Uniqueness of the smooth-point package away from N, q=3
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $q$ is a prime with $q=3$, $M'$ is a nonzero natural number with $q \nmid M'$, and $\ell$ is a prime with $\ell \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$ and $\ell \mid M'$. Further, $A$ is a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ lying over $q$ in the sense of `LiesOverPrime`, i.e. $q$ is a non-unit of $A$; write $\kappa = \mathrm{ResidueField}\,A$. Places are taken in the sense of the project's `Place K F`: a valuation subring of $F$ containing the image of $K$, different from $F$ itself, whose valuation subring is a principal ideal ring; `ord` is the associated normalised integer valuation, `IsRational` says that $K \to$ (residue field of the place) is surjective, and `evalAt` sends $f$ lying in the valuation subring to the preimage in $K$ of its residue (and to $0$ otherwise).
--
--   The data concerning the level-$M'$ situation are: a finite set $W$ of places of $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC}\,\kappa\,M'$ over $\kappa$ (the subfield of $\kappa((q))$ generated by the $q$-expansions `jqModC` and `jqNModC`) together with the hypothesis `hW` that $W$ is exactly `ssPlaces q M' κ`, i.e. the set of places $w$ that are rational, are affine geometric places, and satisfy $w.\mathrm{evalAt}(\mathrm{jGeomGen}\,\kappa\,M') \in \mathrm{ssJSet}\,q\,\kappa$; a chosen element $s \in W$; the inclusion `hle` of $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\,M'$ (the subfield of $\overline{\mathbb Q}((q))$ generated over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ by the coefficientwise images of the full level-$M'$ modular function field over $\mathbb Q$) in $\mathrm{fieldBar}\,q\,M'$, the corresponding base change of the function field of $X_H$ of level $q^2M'$ for $H$ the kernel of $(\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb Z/q)^\times$, i.e. the units congruent to $1$ modulo $q$; and a constant reduction $R_0$ of $A$ on $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\,M'$ with reduced field $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC}\,\kappa\,M'$, that is, a valuation subring $R_0.\mathrm{integers}$ with a surjective residue homomorphism onto that field whose kernel is the maximal ideal, inducing on constants exactly $A$ and its residue map, such that every nonzero element becomes an integral element with nonzero residue after scaling by a constant, and equipped with a map on places preserving degrees and pushing forward divisors of elements with nonzero residue. The hypothesis `hR₀` requires that for every Laurent series $y$ over $A$ whose coefficientwise image in $\overline{\mathbb Q}((q))$ lies in $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\,M'$, this element lies in $R_0.\mathrm{integers}$ and its $R_0$-residue, read as a Laurent series over $\kappa$, is the coefficientwise reduction of $y$.
--
--   The data concerning level $q^2M'$ are: a field $F_{ss}$ which is a $\kappa$-algebra; a regular prolongation $R$ of $A$ on $\mathrm{fieldBar}\,q\,M'$ with reduced field $F_{ss}$ (a valuation subring $R.\mathrm{integers}$ with surjective residue map onto $F_{ss}$ with kernel the maximal ideal, inducing $A$ and its residue map on constants, and with the scaling property above); a finite set $N$ of places of $F_{ss}$ over $\kappa$; and, indexed by the places $Q$ of $F_{ss}$ over $\kappa$, a subring $S_Q \subseteq \mathrm{fieldBar}\,q\,M'$, a ring homomorphism $\varphi_Q : A[T] \to S_Q$, a ring homomorphism $\chi_{0,Q} : S_Q \to \kappa$ and a set $D_Q$ of places of $\mathrm{fieldBar}\,q\,M'$ over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$.
--
--   The hypotheses on these data are the following. `h0`: some element of $F_{ss}$ is transcendental over $\kappa$. `h1` (specialisation at $s$): for every $f \in R_0.\mathrm{integers}$ which is regular wherever $j$ is, in the sense that $\mathrm{ord}_P f \ge 0$ at every place $P$ of $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\,M'$ with $\mathrm{ord}_P$ of the image of the $q$-expansion `jq` non-negative, and whose $R_0$-residue lies in the valuation subring of $s$, the image of $f$ in $\mathrm{fieldBar}\,q\,M'$ lies in $R.\mathrm{integers}$ and its $R$-residue is the image in $F_{ss}$ of $s.\mathrm{evalAt}(R_0.\mathrm{residue}\,f)$. `h2` (level invariance): for every $\zeta$ in $\mathrm{Idx}\,q$, the primitive $q$-th roots of unity in $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, and every $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M') \subseteq \mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb Z)$, the pullback of $R.\mathrm{integers}$ along $\mathrm{levelAutBar}\,q\,M'\,\zeta\,\gamma$ is $R.\mathrm{integers}$. `hcard`: $N$ has exactly $q+1$ elements.
--
--   The hypothesis `hpkg` requires, for every place $Q \notin N$, the following fourteen clauses for $(S_Q, \varphi_Q, \chi_{0,Q}, D_Q)$: (1) the image of every $a \in A$ lies in $S_Q$; (2) $\varphi_Q$ is formally smooth; (3) $\varphi_Q$ is formally unramified; (4) $\varphi_Q(C\,a)$ is the image of $a$ in $\mathrm{fieldBar}\,q\,M'$ for every $a \in A$; (5) $\chi_{0,Q}(\varphi_Q(C\,a))$ is the residue of $a$; (6) $\chi_{0,Q}(\varphi_Q(T)) = 0$; (7) for every $c \in A$ with residue $0$ there is a unique ring homomorphism $\chi : S_Q \to A$ with $\chi(\varphi_Q(C\,a)) = a$ for all $a \in A$, with $\mathrm{residue} \circ \chi = \chi_{0,Q}$, and with $\chi(\varphi_Q(T)) = c$; (8) every $f \in S_Q$ lies in $R.\mathrm{integers}$, its $R$-residue lies in the valuation subring of $Q$, and the residue there of that element is the image of $\chi_{0,Q}(f)$ under $\kappa \to Q.\mathrm{ResidueField}$; (9) $\varphi_Q(T)$ lies in $R.\mathrm{integers}$ and its $R$-residue has $Q$-order $1$; (10) $D_Q$ consists exactly of the places $P$ that are rational, such that every $f \in S_Q$ lies in the valuation subring of $P$ with $P.\mathrm{evalAt}\,f \in A$, and such that for every $f \in S_Q$ one has $A.\mathrm{valuation}(P.\mathrm{evalAt}\,f) < 1$ if and only if $\chi_{0,Q}(f) = 0$; (11) for every ring homomorphism $\chi : S_Q \to A$ which is the identity on the image of $A$ under $\varphi_Q \circ C$ and lifts $\chi_{0,Q}$, there is a unique place $P \in D_Q$ with $P.\mathrm{evalAt}\,f = \chi(f)$ for all $f \in S_Q$; (12) for $P \in D_Q$, an element $f$ lies in the valuation subring of $P$ if and only if there are $g, h \in S_Q$ with $P.\mathrm{evalAt}\,h \neq 0$ and $f h = g$; (13) every nonzero $f$ with $\mathrm{ord}_P f = 0$ for all $P \in D_Q$ becomes a unit of $S_Q$ after multiplication by the image of some nonzero constant of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$; (14) every $f \in R.\mathrm{integers}$ lying in the valuation subring of every $P \in D_Q$ belongs to $S_Q$. The hypothesis `hdisj` requires that for $Q, Q' \notin N$ the sets $D_Q$ and $D_{Q'}$ are disjoint unless $Q = Q'$. The hypothesis `hcusp` requires that for $Q \notin N$ and $P \in D_Q$ the order of the image of `jq` in $\mathrm{fieldBar}\,q\,M'$ at $P$ is non-negative. The hypothesis `heqv` requires that for every $\tau$ in the subgroup of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-algebra automorphisms of $\mathrm{fieldBar}\,q\,M'$ generated by the automorphisms $\mathrm{levelAutBar}\,q\,M'\,\zeta\,\gamma$ with $\zeta \in \mathrm{Idx}\,q$ and $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$, and every witness that $\tau$ preserves $R.\mathrm{integers}$, the induced residue automorphism $R.\mathrm{resAut}\,\tau$ of $F_{ss}$ over $\kappa$ acts on places so that $Q$ lies in $N$ if and only if its translate does, and, for $Q \notin N$, the translated disc $\{P \mid \tau^{-1} \cdot P \in D_Q\}$ equals $D_{Q'}$ for $Q'$ the translate of $Q$.
--
--   Under these hypotheses the conclusion is: for every place $Q \notin N$ and every quadruple consisting of a subring $S \subseteq \mathrm{fieldBar}\,q\,M'$, a ring homomorphism $\varphi : A[T] \to S$, a ring homomorphism $\chi_0 : S \to \kappa$ and a set $D$ of places of $\mathrm{fieldBar}\,q\,M'$ over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ satisfying the same fourteen clauses (1)–(14) with respect to $R$ and $Q$, the following three statements hold: a place $P$ lies in $D$ if and only if it lies in $D_Q$; an element $f$ lies in $S$ if and only if it lies in $S_Q$; and for every $f$ belonging to both $S$ and $S_Q$, the values $\chi_0(f)$ and $\chi_{0,Q}(f)$ in $\kappa$ agree.
--
--   This is the uniqueness half of the theory of smooth-point data on the supersingular fibre at level $q^2M'$ for $q=3$ with the auxiliary rigidifying prime $\ell \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$: it shows that the local ring, the étale coordinate's residue character and the residue disc attached to a place $Q$ of the reduced field outside the exceptional set $N$ are determined by $R$ and $Q$ alone, so that statements formulated with a chosen witness of the corresponding existence theorem do not depend on the choice. It is obtained from the one-sided inclusion of residue discs, [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.supersingularProlongation_smoothPointPackage_disc_subset_of_eq_three_of_dvd`](thm.html#ModularCurve.FullLevel.supersingularProlongation_smoothPointPackage_disc_subset_of_eq_three_of_dvd), and feeds the assembly of prolongation, smooth-point charts, node presentations and the remaining supersingular-fibre data in the $q=3$ case.
